

Full Simulation Test Plan v1.0 — Proton–Laser Interaction & Cosmic Data Integration Engine

1. Purpose

This document defines the first full‑stack simulation test for the Proton–Laser Interaction & Cosmic Data Integration Engine (v1.2).The goal is to validate end‑to‑end functionality across ingestion, preprocessing, proton–laser physics, PIC/MHD evolution, encryption, and HPC execution.

2. Test Scope

This test covers:

Cosmic dataset ingestion

Laser + proton configuration loading

PIC/MHD hybrid solver execution

GPU acceleration layer

Encrypted checkpoint generation

Visualization + analysis pipeline

Integrity verification

It does not cover multi‑scenario sweeps, multi‑GPU scaling, or extended cosmic environments.

3. Test Scenario Definition

3.1 Cosmic Environment

Dataset: Single HDF5 + FITS pair

Components:

ISM density field

cosmic ray spectrum

spectral calibration metadata

3.2 Laser Configuration

Pulse duration: 30 fs

Intensity: 1×10²⁰ W/cm²

Wavelength: 0.8 μm

Focal spot size: 3 μm

3.3 Proton Bunch

Single bunch

Defined initial energy spread

Simple planar geometry

3.4 Expected Physical Behavior

Formation of sheath field

TNSA‑driven proton acceleration

MHD‑coupled magnetic field evolution

Stable density gradients

4. Test Procedure

Step 1 — Ingestion & Preprocessing

Load HDF5 + FITS datasets

Normalize coordinate frames

Extract cosmic boundary conditions

Map ISM density → simulation grid

Load laser + proton configuration

Generate initial state file

Pass Criteria:Initial state file contains coherent cosmic fields, laser parameters, and proton bunch.

Step 2 — Core Simulation Execution

Initialize PIC_SOLVER

Run Vlasov–Maxwell loop

Apply Boris/Vay particle pusher

Interpolate fields (Esirkepov/Yee)

Enable collision operator

Activate MHD coupling

Execute GPU kernels (CUDA/HIP)

Pass Criteria:Simulation runs without solver failure, memory overflow, or divergence.

Step 3 — Encryption & Integrity

Encrypt checkpoint files (AES‑256)

Stream data via TLS 1.3

Generate Merkle tree integrity signatures

Validate signatures across checkpoints

Pass Criteria:Encrypted checkpoints decrypt cleanly and hash validation passes.

Step 4 — Visualization & Analysis

Load output into VTK/ParaView

Render:

proton energy spectrum

sheath field evolution

plasma density slices

magnetic field topology

Compare against analytic expectations or GEANT4 reference

Pass Criteria:Plots show physically plausible acceleration and field behavior.

Step 5 — Stability & Consistency Review

Check for solver divergence

Check for unphysical density spikes

Check for MHD instability

Check for inconsistent checkpoint hashes

Confirm reproducibility across two runs

Pass Criteria:Simulation produces stable, repeatable results with consistent integrity signatures.

5. Pass/Fail Criteria

PASS if:

Engine runs end‑to‑end

Proton acceleration profile is physically plausible

Sheath field forms correctly

MHD coupling behaves consistently

All encrypted checkpoints validate

Visualization pipeline loads without corruption

FAIL if:

Solver diverges

GPU memory bottleneck halts execution

Checkpoint hashes mismatch

Plasma density becomes non‑physical

Laser–proton interaction zone fails to initialize

